.TH OPP 1 2026-10-18 "opp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
opp \- the Oberon pretty printer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B opp
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B opp
reformats Oberon-2 modules written for
.B poc
(the Peaseblossom Oberon Compiler) or
.B voc
(Vishap Oberon), including both compilers' extensions to the language.
It keeps every comment and the author's blank lines, lays the code out
within a maximum line length, and puts statements one per line, packs
short ones onto shared lines, or keeps the author's own grouping.
.PP
With no
.IR file ,
or with
.BR \- ,
opp reads standard input.
It writes each formatted file to standard output in turn, unless
.BR \-\-in\-place ,
.B \-\-output\-dir
or
.B \-\-check
is given.
The text after the period that ends a module is copied as it is.
.PP
Before it writes anything for a file, opp checks its formatted text: the
text must have the input's tokens and comments in the same order, and
must parse.
The only differences allowed are the semicolons of empty statements and
the bars of empty
.B CASE
arms, which opp leaves out, and the bar
.B \-\-case\-bar add
puts before a first arm.
If the check fails, opp reports an internal error and writes nothing for
that file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w " \fIn\fR, " \-\-width " \fIn"
The maximum line length, 20 to 1000; the default is 80.
A line can still be longer when it holds a string, a comment or a name
that does not fit.
.TP
.BR \-i " \fIn\fR, " \-\-indent " \fIn"
Columns per indentation level, 1 to 16; the default is 2.
A continued line is indented two levels.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-one\-per\-line
Put every statement and declaration on its own line, and break every
structured statement over lines.
This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pack
Pack short simple statements, and declarations, onto shared lines while
they fit, and put a structured statement on one line when it fits.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-lines
Keep the author's grouping: statements and declarations share a line
where the source has them on one line, and a structured statement stays
on one line, or broken, as the source has it, while it fits.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-module\-indent
Start a module's declarations at column 1.
The statements after its
.B BEGIN
are still indented.
.TP
.B \-\-module\-indent
Indent a module's declarations, the default: to undo a config file's
.BR no\-module\-indent .
.TP
.BI \-\-case\-bar " what"
What to do with a bar before the first arm of a
.BR CASE :
.B keep
it where the source has one (the default),
.B add
one to every
.BR CASE ,
or
.B drop
it.
.TP
.BI \-\-cuddle\-begin " what"
Whether a procedure's or module's first statement goes on the line of its
.BR BEGIN ,
as
.BR "BEGIN RETURN x" :
.B allow
it where the source has it there (the default),
.B force
it, or
.B disallow
it.
It goes there only when all of the statement fits; the rest of the body
follows on the lines below.
.TP
.BI \-\-cuddle\-end " what"
Whether
.B END
goes on that line too, when the whole body is on it, as
.BR "BEGIN RETURN x END Get" :
.BR allow ,
.B force
or
.BR disallow ,
likewise, and only when all of it fits on the line.
.TP
.BI \-\-align " words"
What to line up, as words separated by commas; a later word wins over an
earlier one, and the option can be given more than once.
.RS
.TP
.BR const ", " type
the
.B =
of constants, or of types;
.TP
.BR var ", " field
the types of variables, or of fields;
with
.B \-p
or
.BR \-k ,
constants, types and variables that share lines go in a grid of
straight columns (below);
.TP
.B init
the
.B :=
of initializers;
.TP
.B comment
trailing comments on lines next to each other;
.TP
.B case
the bodies of
.B CASE
arms on their labels' lines, and of its
.BR ELSE ;
.TP
.B assign
the
.B :=
of assignments, in a run of them up to a blank line or another kind of
statement, each starting its line;
.TP
.B all
all of these;
.TP
.BR no \fIword\fR
one space for that kind of word, as
.B nocomment
or
.BR noall ;
.TP
.BR keep ", " drop
elsewhere, keep the spaces an author lines things up with (the default),
or make each one space.
.RE
.IP
Declarations line up in runs: those of one section or record, up to a
blank line.
A line holding several declarations is left out of its run.
Spaces are kept or added only where they do not make too long a line
that fits without them, and they never change where lines break.
For example,
.B \-\-align all,nocomment
lines up everything but trailing comments.
.IP
With
.B \-p
or
.BR \-k ,
.BR const ,
.B type
and
.B var
put their declarations in a grid: two or more declarations, with no blank
line or comment between them and no comment inside them, in columns with
the same number of declarations on each row, as many as fit, each
column's
.B =
or types lined up.
.B \-k
keeps the source's rows if they fit.
A trailing comment ends its row, and a declaration wider than half the
line, or that cannot be on one line, as a record, has a row of its own.
A variable whose
.B :=
.B init
lines up is left out of grids.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
Replace each file with its formatted text.
A file that is already formatted is not written.
A file is written over, so it keeps its permissions, owner and links; its
old text is first copied to
.IB file .opp\-backup\fR,
which is removed once the file is written, and left, and named, if
writing the file fails.
On VAX/VMS a new version of the file is written instead (see
.BR VAX/VMS ).
Standard input cannot be formatted in place.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " dir"
Write each file's formatted text to the file of the same name in
.IR dir ,
which must exist, creating or replacing it; every file is written, even
one already formatted, and none for a file with a syntax error.
Two files of the same name are a mistake, found before anything is
written, and a file is not written over itself:
.B \-\-in\-place
formats a file in place.
Standard input cannot be written to a directory, and
.B \-\-output\-dir
cannot be used with
.BR \-\-in\-place ,
.B \-\-check
or
.BR \-\-tokens .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-check
Write no formatted text; name each file that is not already formatted,
on standard output, and exit 1 if there is one.
.TP
.B \-\-tokens
List each file's tokens and comments as opp's scanner sees them, instead
of formatting it; a debugging aid.
.TP
.BI \-\-damage " n"
For opp's own tests: spoil the formatted text (1 a token, 2 a comment,
3 the text after the module) to show that the check catches it.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read options from
.I file
instead of from the config files below.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Read no config file.
Of
.B \-\-config
and
.BR \-\-no\-config ,
the last given wins.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage and exit.
.PP
When more than one of
.BR \-1 ,
.B \-p
and
.B \-k
is given, the last one wins.
.SH CONFIG FILES
Before the command line, opp reads options from the user's config file,
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /opp/opp.conf,
or
.I \(ti/.config/opp/opp.conf
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set, and then from the current directory's,
.IR .opp.conf .
A later setting wins over an earlier one: the current directory's file
over the user's, and the command line over both.
Either file may be missing.
.PP
Each line holds one option as the command line gives it, by its long
name, with or without its
.BR \-\- ;
an option's value follows after white space or
.BR = .
A flag, an option that takes no value, is its name alone, as
.B pack
or
.BR no\-module\-indent ;
giving one a value is a mistake.
To turn a flag off, use the option that undoes it, as
.B module\-indent
undoes
.BR no\-module\-indent .
Blank lines are ignored, and
.B #
starts a comment.
Only options that say how to format may be in a config file, not
.BR \-\-in\-place ,
.BR \-\-output\-dir ,
.BR \-\-check ,
.BR \-\-tokens ,
.B \-\-damage
or
.BR \-\-help .
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# \(ti/.config/opp/opp.conf
width = 100
pack
no\-module\-indent
align all,nocomment
.fi
.RE
.PP
A mistake in a config file is reported as
.IB file : line :
.I message
and opp exits with status 2.
.SH VAX/VMS
opp runs on VAX/VMS 5.5\-2, built by poc.
Its options and its formatting are the same as on Unix; what differs is
how VMS names files and gives a program its command line.
.PP
.B OPP.EXE
takes its arguments as a foreign command, so define a symbol for it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ OPP :== $DISK$USER:[TOOLS]OPP.EXE
$ OPP \-\-IN\-PLACE \-P *.MOD
.fi
.RE
.PP
DCL turns what is not quoted into capitals, so opp lowercases every
argument that starts with
.BR \- :
.B \-P
is
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-\-ALIGN=ALL
is
.BR \-\-align=all .
Other arguments are left as they are; VMS does not tell the cases of
file names apart.
DCL does not expand wildcards, so opp does:
.BR * ,
.B %
and
.B ...
name the files they match, each by its whole specification, as
.BR DUA1:[USERS.ME]LEXER.MOD ,
and a pattern that matches none is reported, with exit status 1.
A
.B \-
at the end of a DCL line continues the line, so give standard input as
.B """\-"""
or name no file; and
.B !
starts a comment.
.PP
.B \-\-in\-place
writes the formatted text as a new version of the file, Stream_LF, and
leaves the version it read as it was, as VMS's editors do: that version
is the backup, and
.B PURGE
removes it.
The new version has the old one's protection.
A file of any record format can be formatted, including the
variable-length records that EDT and FTP write.
A file named with a version, as
.BR """LEXER.MOD;3""" ,
is read from that version, and the new version goes above the highest.
.PP
.B \-\-output\-dir
takes a VMS directory, as
.B [.FORMATTED]
or
.BR DUA1:[USERS.ME.OUT] ;
each file is written there under its name and type, without its version,
as a new version if there is one already.
.PP
The user's config file is the one the logical name
.B OPP$CONFIG
names, or else
.BR SYS$LOGIN:OPP.CONF ,
and the current directory's is
.BR OPP.CONF .
.PP
opp's exit status is a VMS condition value, in
.BR $STATUS :
.B SS$_NORMAL
for 0, and for a status
.I n
an error,
.BR %X10000000 " + 8 \(mu " \fIn\fP " + 2" ,
whose message DCL does not print: 1 is
.BR %X1000000A ,
2 is
.B %X10000012
and 3 is
.BR %X1000001A .
A command procedure recovers
.I n
as
.BR "($STATUS .AND. %XFFF8) / 8" .
.PP
VMS writes standard output a record at a time, and a record holds at
most 1024 characters, so a longer line, which only a long comment or
string can make, is broken in two on standard output;
.B \-\-in\-place
and
.B \-\-output\-dir
write files byte for byte and are not affected.
opp counts a line's width as UTF\-8 text, in which the bytes A0 to BF
hexadecimal take no column, so a line with those characters of DEC's
Multinational Character Set can come out wider than the width; the text
itself is never changed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A syntax error or a file that cannot be read or written, or with
.B \-\-check
a file that is not formatted.
.TP
.B 2
A mistake on the command line or in a config file.
.TP
.B 3
An internal error: a formatted text failed the check, and was not
written.
.PP
With several files, every file is still tried, and the worst status
wins.
A syntax error is reported as
.IB file : line : column :
.IR message .
.SH EXAMPLES
Format a module to standard output:
.PP
.RS
.B opp Lexer.Mod
.RE
.PP
Format modules in place, packing short statements, at width 100:
.PP
.RS
.B opp \-p \-w 100 \-\-in\-place *.Mod
.RE
.PP
Write formatted copies of the modules here to a directory:
.PP
.RS
.B opp \-\-output\-dir formatted *.Mod
.RE
.PP
List the modules that are not formatted as the author grouped them:
.PP
.RS
.B opp \-k \-\-check *.Mod
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/opp/opp.conf
The user's config file
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /opp/opp.conf
if that is set).
.TP
.I .opp.conf
The current directory's config file.
.TP
.BR OPP$CONFIG ", " SYS$LOGIN:OPP.CONF ", " OPP.CONF
On VAX/VMS, the user's config file and the current directory's.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR poc (1),
.BR voc (1)
